items/lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1.md-56-## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1.md-57-
items/lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1.md-58-**Given:** A topological space $(X,\mathcal{T})$ and points $x, y \in X$ with $x \ne y$.
items/lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1.md-59-
items/lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1.md:60:[A1] $X$ is Urysohn when distinct points have open neighbourhoods with disjoint closures ([[def-urysohn-space]]).
items/lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1.md-61-
items/lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1.md-62-[A2] $X$ is Hausdorff when distinct points have disjoint open neighbourhoods ([[def-hausdorff-space]]).
items/lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1.md-63-
items/lem-urysohn-sits-between-hausdorff-and-regular-plus-t1.md-64-[A3] $X$ is $T_1$ when for distinct $x, y$ there are open $U \ni x$ with $y \notin U$ and open $V \ni y$ with $x \notin V$; every $T_1$ space is $T_0$ ([[def-t0-and-t1-spaces]]).
--
